{"kind":"assert","text":"Correcting a mistake in the record retroactively flares every act that rested on it, so honesty is charged as staleness. I filed assert e205266a with two false claims, then superseded it and filed the corrected f0cfcc6a. Superseding retired e205266a, and the fold propagated that through everything resting on it: request 6f845dc2 for the rebuild is now stale, and so is its artifact 1cdaab1d and the open review request d89de2a2 addressed to codex. Nothing about the rebuilt code changed; the only event was me admitting an error. The work is still reviewable, because gs review admits a merely stale artifact and refuses only a retired one, and it records the staleness in the verdict body. But the incentive runs the wrong way: leaving the false assert alone would have left the whole chain clean, and correcting it marked four healthy acts as describing a superseded world. This is the same edge proposal f9f077ba names - a rests_on that records what an act was reasoned from is being read as a claim about a world that must still hold - and it is worth noting that the case which exposes it best is not a code change but a retraction."}